@ [Slice6_dependency] specifications/Cantor_Native_Build_And_Artifact_Receipt_P0.sop

& [NativeLifecycleValidationRequest]
  + protocol is cantor.native_lifecycle_validation.v1
  + request identity is a nonempty bounded caller correlation identity with no authority semantics
  + operation is validate_artifact or validate_verification
  + artifact bundle owns exact seed IR candidate plan native request native projection build plan capability sandbox approval trust store authorization observation attempt ledger and artifact receipt forms
  + verification operation additionally owns exact verifier plan observation and receipt
  + artifact operation forbids a verification bundle and verification operation requires one
  + every form remains strict and independently self-validating

& [Replay]
  + validate the request envelope before semantic replay
  + invoke existing seed IR candidate native build capability sandbox approval trust authorization observation ledger artifact and verification validators in causal order
  + do not synthesize, normalize away, repair, sign, execute, or replace any supplied lifecycle form
  + stop at the first invalid dependency and report its stable stage plus typed faults
  + a fully replayed artifact request yields artifact_valid
  + a fully replayed verification request yields verification_valid regardless of whether its valid receipt disposition is passed failed or unresolved

& [NativeLifecycleValidationResponse]
  + protocol echoes exactly
  + request identity and operation echo exactly
  + outcome is artifact_valid verification_valid lifecycle_refused or input_refused
  + deepest_valid_stage names the last successfully replayed stage or none
  + stage account is ordered in canonical causal order with each entered stage exactly once
  + validated artifact identity and digest occur only after artifact receipt replay
  + verification disposition occurs only after verification receipt replay
  + faults are bounded typed and deterministically ordered
  + fixed non-authority denies truth safety authorization execution signing admission installation deployment successor recognition and globally atomic single-use enforcement

& [WireProtocol]
  + UTF-8 JSON only, one object, no trailing non-whitespace data, deny unknown fields at every owned form, maximum input 8388608 bytes
  + normalized compact JSON followed by one line feed on stdout, maximum response 1048576 bytes
  + stdin is default; one --input path may be read; no output path or write surface exists
  + help and version are human-facing stdout modes and do not process semantic input
  + successful valid lifecycle exits 0, lifecycle refusal exits 1, invocation parse limit or wire fault exits 2

& [HonestyAndSecurityBoundary]
  + validity means the representation is internally coherent under the supplied trust roots and exact validators
  + validity does not establish that a reported physical observation occurred, an artifact is correct or safe, a verification passed, or an authority approved later use
  + trust public keys are caller-supplied context; private keys are not part of the schema
  + local attempt-ledger validation does not claim globally atomic authorization consumption

& [EffectBoundary]
  + library validation is pure over supplied values
  + CLI effects are limited to bounded reading of stdin or one named input file and writing response bytes to stdout or diagnostics to stderr
  + no process spawn environment import network clock filesystem write signing installation deployment artifact execution or successor recognition

& [Acceptance]
  + deterministic complete artifact and verification fixtures pass through library and CLI
  + every dependency substitution refuses at or before its owning stage
  + passed failed and unresolved receipts retain exact distinct dispositions
  + malformed trailing unknown oversized wrong-version operation-correspondence and incomplete inputs fail closed
  + full workspace debug and optimized release, warnings-denied lint, format, evidence, and diff gates pass

& [NonGoals]
  + runner, authoring, repair, signing, approval, custody registry, transactional consumption, source generation, install, deploy, execute, internal llama.cpp, FPGA, remote, OneDrive, EVO-X2, and Minecraft

